        <div id="old-vs-new" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Old 20 g vs New 30 g Formula: Buy the Right One
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most important practical point in this review. Happy Viking reformulated in late 2025, but older products have stayed on sale. At the time of writing, Amazon still lists 8-count single-serve packs described as 20 g of protein, including a variety pack and a vanilla pack, alongside the newer Everyday Bags and sampler packs described as 30 g of plant protein with 10 g of fiber.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference is not trivial. The older ready-to-drink Triple Chocolate shake, for example, was listed by grocery retailers at about 20 g of protein and 160 calories per 11 fl oz bottle. Older powders also used erythritol, which the reformulation removed. If you have read an older review that complained about erythritol or a thinner shake, it may not describe what Happy Viking sells now.
          </p>

          <p>
            How to tell them apart: look for 30 g of protein in the listing title or on the front of the pack, and for the Everyday Bag or 10 g fiber language. On Amazon, the current listings are titled Happy Viking Plant-Based Vegan Protein Powder with 100 superfoods or with fiber, while the older packs say Created by Venus Williams, 20G Protein. When in doubt, check the nutrition panel image before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already bought the older 20 g version, it is not a bad product, just a smaller serving. Add a scoop of plain pea protein or blend it with soy milk to bring a shake closer to 30 g. And if you mainly want the convenience of single-serve packs, the current 30 g travel packs are the better buy than the older 8-counts, even if the older ones look cheaper per box.
          </p>
        </div>

